{"qid":"Q5261548","names":["Qasimids"],"title":"Qasimids","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Qasimids — also rendered as the Rassid Qasimid line — were the ruling imams of the Zaydi Shia imamate in Yemen from 1636, when al-Mutawakkil Ismail expelled the Ottomans and unified highland and coastal Yemen for the first time under a single indigenous authority. At their height in the late seventeenth century the Qasimid imams controlled all of Yemen including Aden, Hadramawt, and parts of the Asir region, and their coffee-export economy through Mocha made Yemen central to the global trade that fueled European coffeehouse culture. Internal succession disputes fragmented the imamate by the eighteenth century into competing branches of the Rassid family, and by 1803 effective power had dissolved into regional sultanates. The Zaydi imamate tradition itself persisted through successor lines until the 1962 revolution.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Qasimid imamate's core highland territory is reasonably attested through Yemeni chronicles, but the extent of effective control over coastal and eastern districts fluctuated considerably and boundary reconstructions for the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Rassid dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rassid_dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Yemen history","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Yemen/History"},{"label":"Encyclopaedia Iranica: Zaydiyya","url":"https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/zaydiyya"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Rassid dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name